# podkit — machine-specific configuration.
#
# Copy to `.env.local` and edit. `.env.local` is gitignored and the runtime
# loads it automatically; this file is committed and must never contain a real
# value. Nothing here is needed to run the unit, integration or e2e suites —
# it configures the device *substrate*, which only `test:vm` and the harness
# scripts use.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What belongs here, and what does not
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Here:     WHICH substrate this machine drives, and the credentials that let
#           it lifecycle one.
# Not here: HOW to reach a substrate. Hostname, user, SSH key and any jump host
#           live in your own `~/.ssh/config` under the alias the registry names
#           (`sshAlias` in test-packages/substrate/src/registry.ts). The repo
#           declares capability; your machine declares connection. That split is
#           what keeps a public repository free of anyone's infrastructure by
#           construction rather than by vigilance — see ADR-029 §2.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Substrate selection
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Which substrate the device harness runs on, by registry id.
#
#   device        the local Lima VM (requires `limactl` on PATH)
#   deviceRemote  an SSH-reachable Debian box, resolved through the
#                 `podkit-substrate` alias in your ~/.ssh/config
#
# Leave it unset and selection falls back to the Lima substrate when `limactl`
# is present, ANNOUNCING that it did so, and errors otherwise. The announcement
# matters: if you have a remote substrate configured and forget to select it,
# a silent fallback would hand you a local result that looks like a remote one.
#
# Platform is never consulted. A Mac keeps working with nothing set because
# Lima is installed on it, not because anything special-cases macOS.
#PODKIT_SUBSTRATE=device


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Proxmox VE — substrate lifecycle (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Only needed to CREATE, start, stop, snapshot or destroy a Proxmox-hosted
# substrate from this repo. Everything else — the doctor, binary install, and
# every test suite — works over the SSH link with none of this set; the
# lifecycle verbs then print the equivalent `qm` command for you to run by hand.
#
# The token is privilege-separated and ACL'd to a dedicated pool, so its
# effective rights are the intersection of its user's and its own. Create it
# with the `pveum` recipe in docs/environments/ — never by handing a token the
# root account's privileges.

# Token id, in PVE's `user@realm!tokenname` form.
#PODKIT_PVE_TOKEN_ID=podkit@pve!podkit-substrate

# Token secret — the UUID PVE prints ONCE at creation. A secret, in a gitignored
# file, on one machine. If it ever reaches a commit, revoke it rather than
# rewriting history.
#PODKIT_PVE_TOKEN_SECRET=

# SHA-256 fingerprint of the PVE host's TLS certificate, colon-separated, as
# `openssl x509 -noout -fingerprint -sha256` prints it.
#
# TLS verification is on by default and there is no flag to turn it off. A PVE
# host's certificate is typically self-signed, so pinning its fingerprint is how
# you trust that one host without trusting every certificate that host might
# later present — which is what a blanket insecure switch would buy, permanently
# and for every connection.
#PODKIT_PVE_TLS_FINGERPRINT=
